- 2026-10-20 — **round-12 bounded F2/F3 re-open**. Question: what can repeated pristine controls and declared witnesses establish? Opened existing rows S004, S006 and S026 at their canonical URLs; identified 3, screened 3, included 3, excluded 0. S004 supports reliable copied-tree baselines, S006 supports separating intended from accidental kills, and S026 supplies a caution about rerun masking. No new rows or tier changes; no inference that three matches prove determinism. Stop: both declared claims have scoped primary support. Source quotes and mutable-field changes: `corpus/lessons/round-12.md`, `## Gate`.

- 2026-10-20 — **round-12 scope correction**. The preceding note counts the
  three-source C1/C2 core reading, not the complete wave. Full existing-row
  set opened: S004, S006, S011, S026, S028, S040 — identified 6, screened 6,
  included 5 as scoped claim/lesson support, excluded 1 (S040 remained
  queued; useful corroborating survey, no additional admission claimed).
  No new rows or tier changes. S011/S028 mutable transitions are logged in
  the lesson's source-review addendum; S040 status/recheck/used-by unchanged.
  Stop: the declared two claims have primary support and explicit bounds;
  no broader statistical reliability claim is pursued.

- 2026-10-20 — **round-14 bounded receipt-source reopen**. Question: what does
  an explicit Python child environment establish, and what does a content pin
  omit? Opened Python subprocess docs (new S049) and S003 Limitations;
  identified2/screened2/included2/excluded0. Stop: primary documentation
  answers the invocation contract; neither source proves an experiment ran.
  Quotes and scope are recorded in `corpus/lessons/round-14.md`, Gate.
- 2026-10-20 — **round-15 explicit child invocation**. Reopened S049 official
  Python subprocess docs, run() env paragraph; identified1/screened1/included1/
  excluded0. Stop: the primary API source supports explicit subprocess setup;
  runtime fixtures, not documentation, must demonstrate the context gate.

- 2026-10-20 — **round-16 seventh case and full-page cold-read**. Opened
  Stryker supported-mutators, PIT mutators, kumitate round-gate.mjs @364f1d5,
  Diátaxis compass/how-to/reference, Python doctest, and the Papadakis et al.
  mutation survey; identified6/screened6/included6/excluded0. The survey's
  published chapter (ScienceDirect) returned 403; the UCL author preprint was
  opened instead and is tiered B with that limit. Stop: each claim (operator
  choice, target behaviour, equivalent-mutant handling, section classification,
  executable examples) has one opened primary or tiered source.

## Leads (unopened)

Bullets only — a lead is not evidence and never a row (`tameshi-art.md` leads;
the six opened this round moved to rows S040–S046 and left this list).

- Zeller & Hildebrandt, *Simplifying and Isolating Failure-Inducing Input* (TSE 2002) — doi:10.1109/32.988498.
- Regehr et al., *Test-Case Reduction for C Compiler Bugs* (PLDI 2012) — doi:10.1145/2254064.2254104.
- MacIver, Hatfield-Dodds et al., *Hypothesis: A new approach to property-based testing* (JOSS 2019) — doi:10.21105/joss.01891.
- *A Comprehensive Study on Large Language Models for Mutation Testing* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.09843.
- *Mutation-Guided Unit Test Generation with a Large Language Model* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2506.02954.
- *Detecting Trivial Mutant Equivalences via Compiler Optimisations* (TSE 2018) — https://orbilu.uni.lu/bitstream/10993/31623/1/07882714.pdf.
- Cruz, Rocha, Valente, *Snapshot testing in practice: Benefits and drawbacks* (JSS 2023) — doi:10.1016/j.jss.2023.111797.
- Fujita, Kashiwa, Lin, Iida, *An empirical study on the use of snapshot testing* (ICSME 2023).
- *Flaky Tests in a Large Industrial Database Management System (SAP HANA)* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.03556.
- *On the Flakiness of LLM-Generated Tests for DBMS* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.08998.
- *230,439 Test Failures Later: flaky failure classifiers* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15788.
- C-Vise — https://github.com/marxin/cvise.
- *Trace-Guided Synthesis of Effectful Test Generators* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.04345; *PROGRESS* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2607.27359.
- *Fuzzing with Agents? Generators Are All You Need* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.01442.
- HypoFuzz — https://hypofuzz.com/.
- *What Are Developers Actually Discussing When Visual Regression Tests Fail?* — https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.07020 (adjacent UI domain).

